Rosacea
Dermatology & Soft Tissue
Central facial erythema, Papules/Pustules without comedones, Telangiectasias

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: DST-034
Reason for Visit: Acute, painful, burning facial flare with multiple pustules and severe psychosocial distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a sudden, intensely painful, and burning facial eruption of red bumps and pustules following sun exposure. The patient was in severe psychosocial distress, convinced they had acquired a severe staphylococcal facial infection. Urgent assessment was necessary to differentiate a severe papulopustular rosacea flare from acute facial cellulitis, provide immediate symptom relief for the severe burning, and reassure the patient before the next available daytime appointment.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "My face is completely on fire. It's bright red, burning, and covered in these painful whitehead spots that popped up after I drank wine in the sun yesterday."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a baseline history of persistent central facial erythema and recurrent flushing precipitated by heat or alcohol. However, over the past 24 hours, following a combination of sun exposure and alcohol consumption, they experienced a massive exacerbation. The patient presents with intense burning and a stinging sensation across the cheeks and nose. There has been an acute eruption of multiple erythematous dome-shaped papules and pustules. Crucially, there are absolutely no comedones present. The patient is highly distressed by the burning pain and the disfiguring appearance, fearing a severe bacterial infection. They deny ocular symptoms or rhinophyma.
Functional Status: Independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute psychological distress and embarrassment regarding facial appearance.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/80 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert, well-appearing, but visibly distressed, constantly fanning the face to relieve the burning sensation.
Physical Exam: The central face (cheeks, nose, and chin) demonstrates confluent, striking erythema. Prominent telangiectasias are visible over the cheeks and nose. Superimposed on this erythematous background is a dense eruption of inflammatory, dome-shaped papules and 1-2mm pustules. Careful examination confirms the complete absence of open or closed comedones. There is a complete absence of scaling. Despite the intense erythema and pustules, the skin is not indurated or significantly oedematous, and the borders of the redness are not sharply demarcated. No regional lymphadenopathy is present.
Environmental Observations: Normal home environment.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient applies OTC harsh exfoliating scrubs, which likely exacerbated the compromised skin barrier.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Papulopustular Rosacea - Status: Acute, severe exacerbation
Differentials:

Acne Vulgaris - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The complete absence of comedones (blackheads/whiteheads), the older age of onset, and the prominent background of persistent central facial erythema and telangiectasias.

Facial Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The bilateral, symmetrical nature of the erythema, the lack of systemic fever or malaise, and the absence of a sharply demarcated, indurated, spreading border.

Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (Malar Rash) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of multiple pustules and papules; the classic SLE malar rash is typically a flat or slightly raised erythematous plaque without pustules.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low (benign condition, high distress)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, highly symptomatic flare of papulopustular rosacea, characterized by the classic tetrad of central erythema, telangiectasias, and pustules without comedones. Triage appropriately flagged the severe burning pain and sudden eruption of pustules as requiring evaluation to rule out acute facial cellulitis or a severe infectious process. Immediate out-of-hours clinical assessment confirmed the non-infectious, inflammatory nature of the disease. Providing immediate clinical reassurance, advising cessation of highly irritating topical products, and prescribing oral anti-inflammatory antibiotics rapidly addressed the patient's acute distress and initiated the pathway to calming the intense facial burning.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed oral Doxycycline 50mg daily (sub-antimicrobial dose used strictly for its potent systemic anti-inflammatory effect in rosacea). Instructed the patient to immediately cease all harsh OTC exfoliating scrubs and acid toners. Advised the use of cool compresses for the acute burning sensation.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly identifying and avoiding your personal triggers—in this case, sun exposure and alcohol—you will forcefully prevent the blood vessels in your face from dilating, drastically reducing the intense redness and burning. By switching entirely to a gentle, non-foaming cream cleanser and applying a plain moisturizer daily, you will repair the damaged outer layer of your skin, stopping the painful stinging sensation.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Not required.
DME/Safety (Optional): Not required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 4 weeks to assess the response to Doxycycline and discuss topical therapies (e.g., Ivermectin or Metronidazole cream).

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

If the redness suddenly spreads to your eyelids, if you develop severe pain in your eyes or a gritty feeling, or if the swelling on your face becomes hard and hot with a fever, present to the Emergency Department.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Rosacea is a chronic inflammatory condition. It cannot be permanently cured, but it can be highly successfully managed with medication and trigger avoidance.
Clinical Course: The acute burning and stinging will improve within days as you stop using harsh scrubs. The pustules and red bumps will take 2-4 weeks to clear on the oral medication.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Monitor your eyes closely. Rosacea can occasionally involve the eyes, causing redness and a feeling of sand in the eye, which requires prompt medical review to prevent corneal damage.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Rosacea causes the blood vessels in your face to become hyper-reactive to triggers, leading to massive inflammation and these sterile pustules. The low-dose antibiotic is not being used to kill bacteria; it is being used because it is an excellent anti-inflammatory that will slowly turn off the redness and heal the bumps.
Actionable Steps: Protect your face strictly from the sun with a physical hat and gentle SPF. Do not scrub or pick at the pustules.

